00:44:53.820 --> 00:44:55.280
And he said, I am an actor.

1039
00:44:55.500 --> 00:45:02.020
And the judge, who did not recognize Steve, said, do you do any other work as a day job?

1040
00:45:03.180 --> 00:45:07.060
And he said, no, just the acting.

1041
00:45:08.680 --> 00:45:10.620
And then he got placed on the jury.

1042
00:45:12.180 --> 00:45:16.520
And he deliberated this, I believe it was some sort of civil case.

1043
00:45:17.440 --> 00:45:24.340
Well, Jenna, the last time I went for jury duty, I think it's when Netflix had really kicked it into high gear for the office.

1044
00:45:25.380 --> 00:45:28.280
And they went around and asked us each those questions.

1045
00:45:28.480 --> 00:45:29.740
What is your occupation?

1046
00:45:30.260 --> 00:45:33.000
And I said, I'm an actor.

1047
00:45:33.520 --> 00:45:37.280
And he said, have you ever acted on a law drama?

1048
00:45:37.520 --> 00:45:38.780
The judge said this to me.

1049
00:45:38.880 --> 00:45:39.760
I said, no.

1050
00:45:39.960 --> 00:45:43.780
He said, just you just stick to workplace comedies.

1051
00:45:47.590 --> 00:45:48.630
That's very funny.

1052
00:45:48.850 --> 00:45:49.810
I said, yes.

1053
00:45:49.910 --> 00:45:51.950
And the whole courtroom started laughing.

1054
00:45:52.630 --> 00:45:54.570
Clearly, the judge had watched The Office.

1055
00:45:55.430 --> 00:46:00.190
I know a lot of people always are trying to do these tricks to get out of jury duty.

1056
00:46:00.190 --> 00:46:02.750
But I would love to be on a jury.

1057
00:46:03.030 --> 00:46:05.030
I think the whole thing is fascinating.

1058
00:46:05.590 --> 00:46:06.590
Sign me up.

1059
00:46:06.910 --> 00:46:08.190
You know, it seems great.

1060
00:46:08.670 --> 00:46:11.990
I think that's why I've been picked so many times before The Office.

1061
00:46:12.030 --> 00:46:14.870
Because I actually show up with a notepad and a pen.

1062
00:46:15.010 --> 00:46:15.790
I'm taking notes.

1063
00:46:16.030 --> 00:46:20.650
And I think one side, either the prosecution or defense, is like, we need her.

1064
00:46:20.830 --> 00:46:21.790
She's taking notes.

1065
00:46:22.230 --> 00:46:22.410
Yeah.

1066
00:46:23.170 --> 00:46:28.530
Well, in summary, Valerie Ross is the star of this episode.

1067
00:46:28.830 --> 00:46:29.630
She's incredible.

1068
00:46:30.190 --> 00:46:31.130
She's so good.

1069
00:46:31.350 --> 00:46:33.010
I wanted more stenographer.

1070
00:46:33.330 --> 00:46:34.870
You know what I could listen to?

1071
00:46:35.110 --> 00:46:39.070
I could listen to her read all of Michael Scott's testimony.

1072
00:46:39.670 --> 00:46:42.430
That would be a wonderful extra feature.

1073
00:46:42.810 --> 00:46:45.890
Imagine if Val had read back his opening statement.

1074
00:46:46.710 --> 00:46:48.030
Oh, my gosh.

1075
00:46:49.050 --> 00:46:51.710
Oh, I want to contact her and see if she'll do it.

1076
00:46:53.090 --> 00:46:54.010
That's amazing.

1077
00:46:54.610 --> 00:46:56.610
Should we go back to Scranton, Angela?

1078
00:46:57.610 --> 00:47:01.630
Jim has just beaten Meredith at ping pong in the conference room.

1079
00:47:01.930 --> 00:47:05.850
And Dwight busts in and he demands to know what is going on in here.

1080
00:47:05.850 --> 00:47:10.090
Well, Jim says he's got a big client that plays ping pong and he needs to, like, impress him.

1081
00:47:10.270 --> 00:47:11.470
So Dwight's like, I'll help you.

1082
00:47:11.710 --> 00:47:12.150
Right?

1083
00:47:12.650 --> 00:47:13.090
Yes.

1084
00:47:13.250 --> 00:47:17.490
And he's got this talking head where Dwight says all of his heroes are table tennis players.

1085
00:47:17.830 --> 00:47:18.530
Who knew?

1086
00:47:18.830 --> 00:47:21.990
And he lists all of these names of actual people.

1087
00:47:22.390 --> 00:47:27.950
And in the commentary, Rain shared that they actually put those names on a cue card for him.

1088
00:47:28.270 --> 00:47:31.250
A big cue card so that he didn't have to memorize them.

1089
00:47:31.370 --> 00:47:33.270
And he was able to read them off.

1090
00:47:33.470 --> 00:47:34.730
Well, I'll tell you what, Angela.

1091
00:47:34.930 --> 00:47:35.990
We had a fan catch.

1092
00:47:36.210 --> 00:47:36.610
What?

1093
00:47:36.830 --> 00:47:38.910
From Macy Gessler, who said,

1094
00:47:39.090 --> 00:47:46.970
You can see a white square in the reflection of Rain's glasses during that talking head.

1095
00:47:47.330 --> 00:47:50.310
That is the reflection of the cue card.

1096
00:47:50.310 --> 00:47:53.010
That is such a great catch.

1097
00:47:53.310 --> 00:47:54.070
Mm-hmm.

1098
00:47:54.310 --> 00:47:57.190
And I paused and I looked at it and sure enough, it's there.

1099
00:47:57.770 --> 00:47:58.070
Really?

1100
00:47:58.610 --> 00:47:59.110
Aw, dang.

1101
00:47:59.110 --> 00:48:00.370
I got to go back and look at that.

1102
00:48:00.470 --> 00:48:01.210
That's so good.

1103
00:48:01.650 --> 00:48:04.110
Big moment happening over at corporate.

1104
00:48:04.650 --> 00:48:10.770
The deposition is going off the rails because this is when there's a lot of questioning about

1105
00:48:10.770 --> 00:48:14.370
when did Jan and Michael's relationship really begin?

1106
00:48:14.710 --> 00:48:20.070
And Michael has testified to one thing and they try to prove him wrong.

1107
00:48:20.310 --> 00:48:28.150
By showing this picture of Jan on the beach in Jamaica, callback to back from vacation that

1108
00:48:28.150 --> 00:48:35.030
Michael accidentally sent to everyone as proof that their relationship started before the

1109
00:48:35.030 --> 00:48:36.490
love document says it did.

1110
00:48:36.970 --> 00:48:40.170
Jan's lawyer says that photo actually isn't proof.

1111
00:48:40.170 --> 00:48:43.690
And we have Michael's journal to prove it.

1112
00:48:43.930 --> 00:48:45.990
And Michael is like, what?

1113
00:48:46.570 --> 00:48:53.230
Jan brought Michael's personal journal and they begin to read out of it.

1114
00:48:53.330 --> 00:48:55.110
It's so embarrassing for Michael.

1115
00:48:55.590 --> 00:48:58.510
Well, he's written all about his trip to Jamaica with Jan.

1116
00:48:58.810 --> 00:49:02.670
And it's written like a 13-year-old boy wrote it.

1117
00:49:03.170 --> 00:49:07.230
It's written, Jenna, the way I would write in my journal, like when I was eight years old.

1118
00:49:07.450 --> 00:49:08.170
Dear diary.

1119
00:49:08.390 --> 00:49:10.210
And then at the end, I'd be like, XOXO.

1120
00:49:10.450 --> 00:49:11.210
Like, what am I?

1121
00:49:11.290 --> 00:49:12.250
Is the diary a person?

1122
00:49:12.810 --> 00:49:13.250
Yeah.

1123
00:49:13.470 --> 00:49:14.710
So it's like that.

1124
00:49:14.710 --> 00:49:18.270
Or it's like me writing about meeting Patrick Dempsey.

1125
00:49:19.130 --> 00:49:20.010
Very similar.

1126
00:49:21.710 --> 00:49:23.090
You know, Jenna and I journal.

1127
00:49:23.310 --> 00:49:24.410
You guys, you know this, right?

1128
00:49:24.630 --> 00:49:27.150
We shared the Patrick Dempsey journal on this podcaSt.

1129
00:49:27.510 --> 00:49:27.850
Yes.

1130
00:49:28.030 --> 00:49:28.990
We're big journalers.

1131
00:49:29.130 --> 00:49:31.170
I counted actually how many journals I have.

1132
00:49:32.070 --> 00:49:33.690
I don't even think I could count.

1133
00:49:34.030 --> 00:49:35.410
I've got over two dozen.

1134
00:49:35.510 --> 00:49:36.670
I mean, so many.

1135
00:49:36.990 --> 00:49:37.670
So many.

1136
00:49:37.910 --> 00:49:38.970
I have 15.

1137
00:49:40.230 --> 00:49:41.010
Oh, wow.

1138
00:49:41.010 --> 00:49:44.090
Well, you know, lady, I did The Artist's Way.

1139
00:49:44.390 --> 00:49:49.530
It's a 12-week self-led creativity seminar in a book.

1140
00:49:49.710 --> 00:49:51.170
It's amazing if you're an artiSt.

1141
00:49:51.470 --> 00:49:58.310
But it requires you to journal three pages every single morning, first thing.

1142
00:49:58.510 --> 00:50:07.270
So I have legal pads and legal pads of these handwritten journals from when I did The Artist's

1143
00:50:07.270 --> 00:50:07.370
Way.

1144
00:50:07.510 --> 00:50:10.610
And I did The Artist's Way twice because the first time I didn't finish it.

1145
00:50:11.010 --> 00:50:13.390
So I have, like, so much journal.

1146
00:50:14.070 --> 00:50:17.990
Here is the journal entry that Jan's lawyer reads.

1147
00:50:18.530 --> 00:50:22.170
I quote from an entry dated January 4 of this past year.

1148
00:50:22.550 --> 00:50:24.130
Just got back from Jamaica.

1149
00:50:24.570 --> 00:50:25.990
Tan almost everywhere.

1150
00:50:26.390 --> 00:50:27.930
Jan almost everywhere.

1151
00:50:28.210 --> 00:50:28.690
He he.

1152
00:50:29.150 --> 00:50:30.990
Oh, diary, what a week.

1153
00:50:31.130 --> 00:50:32.910
I had sex with my boss.

1154
00:50:33.130 --> 00:50:35.090
I don't know if it's going to go anywhere.

1155
00:50:35.370 --> 00:50:40.770
Jan was very specific that it is not going anywhere, that it was a one-time mistake.

1156
00:50:41.010 --> 00:50:43.550
But we had sex six times.

1157
00:50:43.730 --> 00:50:44.510
So you tell me.

1158
00:50:44.690 --> 00:50:47.990
I am definitely feeling very iry.

1159
00:50:48.210 --> 00:50:49.510
But he says eerie.

1160
00:50:50.010 --> 00:50:51.150
And then Michael corrects him.

1161
00:50:52.550 --> 00:50:54.210
Oh, more tomorrow.

1162
00:50:54.830 --> 00:50:55.970
XOXO, Michael.

1163
00:50:56.910 --> 00:50:57.350
Incredible.

1164
00:50:57.830 --> 00:51:00.510
Angela, I looked up the definition of iry.

1165
00:51:00.810 --> 00:51:01.590
You did?

1166
00:51:01.950 --> 00:51:02.470
Yeah.

1167
00:51:02.900 --> 00:51:05.130
The lawyer thinks he meant eerie.

1168
00:51:05.390 --> 00:51:07.470
But Michael's like, it's iry.

1169
00:51:07.610 --> 00:51:08.730
He's been in Jamaica.

1170
00:51:09.150 --> 00:51:09.730
Yes.

1171
00:51:10.010 --> 00:51:13.430
And it means nice, good, or pleasing.

1172
00:51:13.430 --> 00:51:17.510
And it is used as a general term of approval in Jamaican English.

1173
00:51:18.090 --> 00:51:21.570
So despite everything, he's feeling pretty iry.

1174
00:51:22.250 --> 00:51:28.790
I have to say, Michael is absolutely crushed and feels betrayed by this reading of his journal.

1175
00:51:29.030 --> 00:51:31.570
It is embarrassing, and he is right to feel that way.

1176
00:51:31.770 --> 00:51:32.630
Jan is horrible.

1177
00:51:32.930 --> 00:51:35.690
This was horrible, and this was premeditated.

1178
00:51:35.950 --> 00:51:36.330
Yeah.

1179
00:51:36.530 --> 00:51:38.270
She brought it from their home.

1180
00:51:38.450 --> 00:51:41.450
Now they decide, we're going to make copies of the journal.

1181
00:51:41.670 --> 00:51:43.050
We're going to make 10 copies.

1182
00:51:43.250 --> 00:51:44.690
Toby's like, actually, make it 11.

1183
00:51:45.250 --> 00:51:50.130
Michael is just sinking further and further into this, like, embarrassing spiral.

1184
00:51:50.410 --> 00:51:54.890
And Lester talked on the DVD commentary about one of the challenges of writing this episode

1185
00:51:54.890 --> 00:51:58.550
was taking Michael through these layers, right?

1186
00:51:58.770 --> 00:52:01.750
At the beginning of the deposition, he's feeling confident.

1187
00:52:01.970 --> 00:52:05.190
And slowly with each layer, it's just all falling apart.

1188
00:52:05.470 --> 00:52:09.890
It's hard to believe that it's going to get worse from here, and yet it does.

1189
00:52:10.050 --> 00:52:11.050
It's so tragic.

1190
00:52:11.050 --> 00:52:18.550
And now they go to lunch, and everyone in this corporate lunchroom is reading his journal.

1191
00:52:18.870 --> 00:52:22.570
All I could think of was, corporate has a cafeteria?

1192
00:52:24.630 --> 00:52:28.290
I believed it, Jenna, because you know those big buildings in New York?

1193
00:52:28.470 --> 00:52:29.090
I do.

1194
00:52:29.450 --> 00:52:31.570
They have cafeterias and food areas.

1195
00:52:31.950 --> 00:52:35.070
Lady, I love a corporate cafeteria.

1196
00:52:35.470 --> 00:52:36.490
I'll tell you what.

1197
00:52:36.490 --> 00:52:42.050
I only experienced it once when I worked at the Coca-Cola bottling company in Los Angeles.

1198
00:52:42.210 --> 00:52:44.090
It's where they bottle the soda.

1199
00:52:44.330 --> 00:52:46.830
I worked on the complaint hotline there.

1200
00:52:47.050 --> 00:52:47.770
You did?

1201
00:52:48.090 --> 00:52:49.010
I did.

1202
00:52:49.270 --> 00:52:56.050
I took all of the complaints, and basically the policy was anyone who called to complain

1203
00:52:56.050 --> 00:52:57.990
got free coupons for soda.

1204
00:52:58.230 --> 00:53:02.950
I had to listen to all the complaints and log all the complaints, and then mail the coupons.

1205
00:53:02.990 --> 00:53:03.930
That was my job.

1206
00:53:03.930 --> 00:53:06.610
It's kind of off in a remote area, this factory.

1207
00:53:06.950 --> 00:53:08.710
You get 30 minutes for lunch.

1208
00:53:08.910 --> 00:53:14.090
There's nowhere to go, so you had to bring your lunch, or they had a corporate cafeteria.

1209
00:53:14.630 --> 00:53:18.190
And lady, all the soda was free.

1210
00:53:18.510 --> 00:53:18.870
Ho!

1211
00:53:19.430 --> 00:53:21.810
You could drink as much as you wanted all day.

1212
00:53:22.150 --> 00:53:25.030
The fountain sodas were free.

1213
00:53:25.530 --> 00:53:27.490
But I love a cafeteria.

1214
00:53:27.910 --> 00:53:29.050
I don't know what it is about it.

1215
00:53:29.150 --> 00:53:30.610
I liked it when I was in high school.

1216
00:53:30.610 --> 00:53:32.130
I loved it in college.

1217
00:53:32.530 --> 00:53:33.930
I love a cafeteria.

1218
00:53:34.610 --> 00:53:36.890
This is going to melt your brain for a moment.

1219
00:53:37.170 --> 00:53:43.810
About a year and a half ago, I met my friend, Derek Haas, who works on Chicago Fire, Chicago

1220
00:53:43.810 --> 00:53:44.110
PD.

1221
00:53:44.450 --> 00:53:45.570
He's like the showrunner creator.

1222
00:53:45.730 --> 00:53:47.550
He's like amazing, talented writer.

1223
00:53:47.830 --> 00:53:48.870
We went to college together.

1224
00:53:49.070 --> 00:53:51.030
He's like, Ange, come have lunch.

1225
00:53:51.110 --> 00:53:51.730
Let's catch up.

1226
00:53:51.730 --> 00:53:52.710
I'm like, awesome.

1227
00:53:53.130 --> 00:53:56.190
He's like, you know, our office is on Universal Studio Lot.

1228
00:53:56.390 --> 00:53:56.990
I said, yeah.

1229
00:53:57.210 --> 00:53:58.490
He was like, so come meet me.

1230
00:53:58.550 --> 00:54:02.370
We'll go to the NBC Universal Commissary.

1231
00:54:02.650 --> 00:54:03.510
I said, what?

1232
00:54:03.870 --> 00:54:04.950
He said, have you never been?

1233
00:54:05.330 --> 00:54:06.750
You're on an NBC show.

1234
00:54:07.310 --> 00:54:12.430
I was like, no, we filmed over in this little soundstage in the middle of nowhere.

1235
00:54:12.730 --> 00:54:15.850
He said, oh yeah, NBC has an amazing commissary.

1236
00:54:16.590 --> 00:54:17.030
What?

1237
00:54:18.070 --> 00:54:20.510
Jenna, it looks like you're at the Four Seasons.

1238
00:54:20.510 --> 00:54:23.190
I'm talking like fabric napkin.

1239
00:54:23.450 --> 00:54:28.430
The water glasses that are the big glasses, you know, where your iced tea comes with like

1240
00:54:28.430 --> 00:54:29.410
a lemon wedge.

1241
00:54:30.050 --> 00:54:30.230
Yeah.

1242
00:54:30.710 --> 00:54:34.970
Jenna, we never got the NBC Commissary treatment, but it was really good.

1243
00:54:35.350 --> 00:54:40.770
I also went there years, years later to have lunch with Terry Weinberg, who is one of the

1244
00:54:40.770 --> 00:54:42.670
executive producers of The Office.

1245
00:54:43.130 --> 00:54:44.630
She works on the lot.

1246
00:54:44.930 --> 00:54:48.330
She invited me for lunch at the commissary, and I thought the same thing.

1247
00:54:48.330 --> 00:54:56.390
I thought, wow, Greg could have chosen to have our show on the lot, but he wanted us to

1248
00:54:56.390 --> 00:55:01.270
be more independent and have our own space, which ultimately I loved and was the right

1249
00:55:01.270 --> 00:55:01.770
decision.

1250
00:55:02.650 --> 00:55:04.390
Except for that damn cafeteria.

1251
00:55:04.930 --> 00:55:06.630
I mean, I would have really liked that.

1252
00:55:06.850 --> 00:55:08.370
That cafeteria was legit.

1253
00:55:08.830 --> 00:55:13.030
This is not to say that we didn't have the greatest food on our set because our caterers

1254
00:55:13.030 --> 00:55:13.650
were amazing.

1255
00:55:13.650 --> 00:55:16.230
I just really like a cafeteria experience.

1256
00:55:16.230 --> 00:55:18.170
It's not the food, necessarily.

1257
00:55:18.170 --> 00:55:22.930
I love the tray and you go down the little thing with the silver bars.

1258
00:55:23.090 --> 00:55:24.630
You know, I love a soup plantation.

1259
00:55:24.930 --> 00:55:26.030
This is why, I think.

1260
00:55:26.610 --> 00:55:28.830
Lady, you don't have to even talk about this with me.

1261
00:55:28.870 --> 00:55:31.130
You've heard my thoughts on a Vegas buffet.

1262
00:55:31.470 --> 00:55:32.010
I know.

1263
00:55:32.550 --> 00:55:34.170
It's just things we like.

1264
00:55:34.670 --> 00:55:38.690
Guess where we filmed these corporate cafeteria scenes, Angela?

1265
00:55:38.990 --> 00:55:40.870
I know, but I'm not going to say.

1266
00:55:41.050 --> 00:55:41.710
You tell us.

1267
00:55:41.710 --> 00:55:45.590
We filmed them at the Universal Studios Commissary.

1268
00:55:46.190 --> 00:55:47.190
So crazy.

1269
00:55:47.470 --> 00:55:49.290
We filmed them for one day.

1270
00:55:49.470 --> 00:55:53.270
We rented the entire space so everyone who was working there didn't get to eat lunch there

1271
00:55:53.270 --> 00:55:57.950
that day because all the people in the background were hired by us for non-speaking roles.

1272
00:55:58.130 --> 00:56:03.150
We had that cafeteria that day to shoot these lunch scenes.

1273
00:56:03.550 --> 00:56:07.390
Well, Jenna, Michael is walking around the cafeteria and he doesn't know where to sit.

1274
00:56:07.690 --> 00:56:08.830
Everyone's reading his journal.

1275
00:56:08.830 --> 00:56:14.830
Now, he's feeling a bit lost and you know he's at the bottom of the barrel when he goes

1276
00:56:14.830 --> 00:56:15.730
and sits with Toby.

1277
00:56:16.170 --> 00:56:17.730
And Toby tries.

1278
00:56:18.230 --> 00:56:20.030
Toby is so kind.

1279
00:56:20.570 --> 00:56:20.950
Yeah.

1280
00:56:21.090 --> 00:56:25.030
He brings up his parents' divorce and how it made him feel.

1281
00:56:25.290 --> 00:56:27.190
Both of his parents wanted him to testify.

1282
00:56:27.190 --> 00:56:29.670
And he's like, but I just love them both so much.

1283
00:56:30.870 --> 00:56:37.130
And he's sort of saying, Michael, I know you love Jan and you love Dunder Mifflin, so I see

1284
00:56:37.130 --> 00:56:39.030
how you're pulled in two directions too.

1285
00:56:39.150 --> 00:56:40.230
That's the subtext.

1286
00:56:40.790 --> 00:56:43.870
Michael responds by pushing Toby's tray onto the floor.

1287
00:56:44.010 --> 00:56:45.890
And his entire lunch goes with it.

1288
00:56:46.190 --> 00:56:48.170
So Lester talked about this moment.

1289
00:56:48.370 --> 00:56:54.390
He said in the script they had written where Michael just goes off on Toby, like makes a big

1290
00:56:54.390 --> 00:56:54.890
speech.

1291
00:56:55.070 --> 00:56:56.330
And they filmed it that way.

1292
00:56:56.450 --> 00:57:00.510
And then Paul Lieberstein pitched, hey, why don't we do one take where he just shoves

1293
00:57:00.510 --> 00:57:03.950
my tray off the table and walks away and doesn't even respond to me.

1294
00:57:04.230 --> 00:57:05.130
And they did it.

1295
00:57:05.270 --> 00:57:07.790
It was just like in the moment, idea in the moment.

1296
00:57:08.010 --> 00:57:09.210
And that's what made it in.

1297
00:57:09.450 --> 00:57:10.190
So good.

1298
00:57:10.550 --> 00:57:11.370
So funny.

1299
00:57:11.730 --> 00:57:17.970
When the deposition continues, they are still reading Michael's diary.

1300
00:57:18.370 --> 00:57:19.310
They have some questions.

1301
00:57:19.310 --> 00:57:23.310
They say, who is this other woman, Ryan, who you refer to?

1302
00:57:24.190 --> 00:57:24.730
No.

1303
00:57:25.850 --> 00:57:27.870
Toby cannot keep his composure.

1304
00:57:28.270 --> 00:57:30.070
He is cracking up.

1305
00:57:31.410 --> 00:57:35.170
Michael just so looks up to Ryan.

1306
00:57:35.450 --> 00:57:40.950
And his admiration of Ryan is one of my favorite little tidbits of the show.

1307
00:57:41.190 --> 00:57:43.110
Well, he has a man crush on him.

1308
00:57:43.230 --> 00:57:46.170
And, you know, Ryan got to do all the things I think Michael had hoped for.

1309
00:57:46.310 --> 00:57:47.290
He went to college.

1310
00:57:47.290 --> 00:57:48.550
He got his MBA.

1311
00:57:48.550 --> 00:57:49.950
He's at corporate now.

1312
00:57:50.170 --> 00:57:54.570
He represents something to Michael that Michael always hoped to have.

1313
00:57:54.750 --> 00:57:55.090
Yeah.

1314
00:57:55.270 --> 00:57:56.250
That kind of status.

1315
00:57:56.970 --> 00:58:02.610
And now they're going to start reading from Jan's performance review of Michael,

1316
00:58:02.790 --> 00:58:05.830
which we find out she wrote after they were together.

1317
00:58:06.570 --> 00:58:07.130
Oh.

1318
00:58:07.490 --> 00:58:13.070
And her comments are so harsh and mean, really mean.

1319
00:58:13.630 --> 00:58:16.770
She recommends that he be let go from the company.

1320
00:58:16.770 --> 00:58:18.330
She's out of carrots.

1321
00:58:18.330 --> 00:58:19.490
She's out of sticks.

1322
00:58:19.950 --> 00:58:20.150
Yeah.

1323
00:58:20.690 --> 00:58:24.950
Nothing works with Michael and that he's best suited for sales, which, by the way, Jenna,

1324
00:58:25.050 --> 00:58:26.130
I don't think she's wrong.

1325
00:58:26.390 --> 00:58:27.750
I don't think she's wrong either.

1326
00:58:28.250 --> 00:58:30.090
But this guts Michael.

1327
00:58:30.530 --> 00:58:30.790
Yeah.

1328
00:58:31.270 --> 00:58:36.150
He said, you expect to get screwed by your company, but you don't expect to get screwed

1329
00:58:36.150 --> 00:58:36.990
by your girlfriend.

1330
00:58:36.990 --> 00:58:38.670
We got some mail.

1331
00:58:38.890 --> 00:58:39.450
We did.

1332
00:58:39.650 --> 00:58:46.430
This is from Lu Chen, who says, as a lawyer who's taken depositions, I felt that the Michael

1333
00:58:46.430 --> 00:58:51.490
deposition as depicted in this episode was impressively true to life while still being

1334
00:58:51.490 --> 00:58:51.990
hilarious.

1335
00:58:52.790 --> 00:58:54.310
And Kate P.

1336
00:58:54.590 --> 00:58:55.470
and Peter W.

1337
00:58:55.730 --> 00:58:57.650
pointed out something I thought was very interesting.

1338
00:58:57.650 --> 00:59:03.490
Why didn't they use the documentary film footage for proof in the deposition?

1339
00:59:04.610 --> 00:59:06.290
Oh, interesting.

1340
00:59:06.890 --> 00:59:07.610
Yes.

1341
00:59:08.150 --> 00:59:08.950
Interesting.

1342
00:59:09.530 --> 00:59:10.750
They would have.

1343
00:59:10.970 --> 00:59:12.110
They could have.

1344
00:59:12.550 --> 00:59:12.990
Yeah.

1345
00:59:13.510 --> 00:59:15.630
We could see a lot of stuff on tape.

1346
00:59:15.890 --> 00:59:17.350
They didn't ask.

1347
00:59:18.070 --> 00:59:18.630
Hmm.

1348
00:59:18.790 --> 00:59:21.650
But they agreed to have the cameras in the room.

1349
00:59:22.090 --> 00:59:22.790
Yes.

1350
00:59:24.230 --> 00:59:25.350
So interesting.

1351
00:59:27.350 --> 00:59:31.850
Well, you know, Jenna, in the deleted scenes, there's a moment, you know, where Michael's

1352
00:59:31.850 --> 00:59:34.070
like, listen, she didn't mean it.

1353
00:59:34.210 --> 00:59:36.510
She was going through a divorce at the time.

1354
00:59:36.590 --> 00:59:38.430
She was drinking a lot.

1355
00:59:38.930 --> 00:59:41.090
And then Jan makes a face, right?

1356
00:59:41.610 --> 00:59:46.090
And then Michael goes, of water, because she was taking all of those pills.

1357
00:59:47.790 --> 00:59:52.490
He just keeps revealing all of the stuff that's making it worse and worse.

1358
00:59:52.790 --> 00:59:54.890
And they had to trim some of that for time.

1359
00:59:55.030 --> 00:59:56.730
But I just loved it.

1360
00:59:57.290 --> 01:00:01.970
Ladies, speaking of water, do you know how much water Tom Brady drinks every day?

1361
01:00:03.030 --> 01:00:03.310
What?

1362
01:00:03.690 --> 01:00:03.950
Yeah.

1363
01:00:04.270 --> 01:00:04.830
Tom Brady?

1364
01:00:05.110 --> 01:00:06.070
Where is this coming from?

1365
01:00:06.290 --> 01:00:09.670
It's coming from the fact that the Super Bowl was recently.

1366
01:00:10.170 --> 01:00:10.390
Yeah.

1367
01:00:10.850 --> 01:00:12.430
And Tom Brady won.

1368
01:00:12.690 --> 01:00:13.090
Yeah.

1369
01:00:13.190 --> 01:00:15.510
And he looks like he's still 24.

1370
01:00:16.490 --> 01:00:20.910
And I became a little obsessed with his athletic ability.

1371
01:00:20.910 --> 01:00:24.790
And I read, and this is common knowledge, apparently.

1372
01:00:25.050 --> 01:00:28.710
Lee was like, welcome to Tom Brady, Jenna.

1373
01:00:29.010 --> 01:00:29.490
Thanks for.

1374
01:00:29.750 --> 01:00:30.670
I don't know it.

1375
01:00:30.710 --> 01:00:31.170
What is it?

1376
01:00:31.270 --> 01:00:35.930
He has this very, very, very strict diet that he adheres to.

1377
01:00:36.150 --> 01:00:37.330
He has a personal chef.

1378
01:00:37.630 --> 01:00:40.610
And he eats this mostly plant-based diet.

1379
01:00:40.610 --> 01:00:45.790
But it also involves drinking gallons and gallons and gallons of water a day.

1380
01:00:45.790 --> 01:00:48.190
Like half his body weight in water.

1381
01:00:48.610 --> 01:00:53.830
And all I could think about was, number one, this is such a great advertisement for drinking water.

1382
01:00:54.030 --> 01:00:59.310
But number two, how does he make it through a whole football game without having to pee constantly?

1383
01:00:59.730 --> 01:01:01.150
How does he do it?

1384
01:01:01.750 --> 01:01:05.410
I drink eight glasses of water a day, and I feel like all I do is pee.

1385
01:01:05.750 --> 01:01:07.470
How does he drink so much water?

1386
01:01:08.050 --> 01:01:13.250
And every time now, ever since learning this, that anyone brings up water, I think of Tom Brady.

1387
01:01:14.230 --> 01:01:16.250
Well, maybe he does pee during a game.

1388
01:01:16.590 --> 01:01:18.350
Maybe it just goes down his leg.

1389
01:01:18.490 --> 01:01:19.470
I mean, you would never know.

1390
01:01:19.710 --> 01:01:20.690
Oh, my God.

1391
01:01:21.070 --> 01:01:21.450
Angela.

1392
01:01:22.270 --> 01:01:27.310
Listen, I would have thought that was crazy until, you know, Josh, who played soccer.

1393
01:01:27.470 --> 01:01:30.550
Josh was a big soccer player all through high school, and he played in college.

1394
01:01:30.550 --> 01:01:32.610
And he said, listen to this.

1395
01:01:32.830 --> 01:01:41.830
He said on a soccer field during a long game, sometimes you can just, like, take a knee and whip it out to the side and just pee for a second.

1396
01:01:42.190 --> 01:01:45.310
So those soccer games, the guys are peeing.

1397
01:01:45.910 --> 01:01:50.270
So when they're, like, they're pretending to maybe lace their shoe or something?

1398
01:01:50.450 --> 01:01:50.950
Yeah, yeah.

1399
01:01:50.950 --> 01:01:53.510
They just flop it out and take a whiz.

1400
01:01:53.910 --> 01:01:56.990
But football players wear super tight pants and stuff.

1401
01:01:56.990 --> 01:01:58.750
I feel like you would see it.

1402
01:01:58.870 --> 01:01:59.850
No, you wouldn't.

1403
01:01:59.850 --> 01:02:00.710
It's all padded.

1404
01:02:00.850 --> 01:02:01.590
You wouldn't see it.

1405
01:02:01.910 --> 01:02:03.610
I bet they can pee in their pants.

1406
01:02:04.110 --> 01:02:05.150
Oh, my gosh.

1407
01:02:05.210 --> 01:02:06.070
I need to know.

1408
01:02:06.550 --> 01:02:08.090
Football players, someone write in.

1409
01:02:08.170 --> 01:02:10.610
Have you ever peed during a game and did anyone know?

1410
01:02:11.710 --> 01:02:20.470
Also, person who interviews Tom Brady next about his diet and about his water consumption, will you please ask him about this?

1411
01:02:20.510 --> 01:02:25.130
Because I need to understand when and how often the man goes to the bathroom.

1412
01:02:25.510 --> 01:02:26.630
I need to know.

1413
01:02:26.910 --> 01:02:28.410
This seems unmanageable.

1414
01:02:29.850 --> 01:02:30.670
Here's my takeaway.

1415
01:02:31.110 --> 01:02:34.610
Don't go on a road trip with Tom Brady unless you like making lots of stops.

1416
01:02:37.520 --> 01:02:40.220
If we want to continue the Tom Brady tangent,

1417
01:02:40.240 --> 01:02:48.620
I remember one time Lee and I read about the new house that he and Giselle purchased and it was humongous and it had like a gazillion rooMs.

1418
01:02:48.880 --> 01:02:51.740
And Lee said something that was so funny to me.

1419
01:02:51.740 --> 01:03:01.460
He said, can you imagine if Tom Brady gets to the kitchen and he realizes he left his cell phone in the bedroom, how far he has to walk to get it?

1420
01:03:01.680 --> 01:03:08.180
He's like, I get annoyed when I have to walk because I forgot something in another room.

1421
01:03:08.180 --> 01:03:11.680
But think of how far they have to walk.

1422
01:03:12.680 --> 01:03:14.520
And it's always stuck with me.

1423
01:03:14.720 --> 01:03:15.920
Because it's true.

1424
01:03:16.260 --> 01:03:17.580
You know what they do?

1425
01:03:17.900 --> 01:03:19.200
They go like this.

1426
01:03:19.660 --> 01:03:21.480
And someone goes and gets it for them.

1427
01:03:21.620 --> 01:03:23.300
Oh, they have a staff.

1428
01:03:23.600 --> 01:03:24.480
They have a staff.

1429
01:03:24.860 --> 01:03:26.160
That's what it is.

1430
01:03:26.260 --> 01:03:27.440
The butler gets it.

1431
01:03:27.880 --> 01:03:30.980
Tom Brady doesn't walk back to his bedroom for his cell phone.

1432
01:03:30.980 --> 01:03:32.740
He's too busy drinking water.

1433
01:03:33.060 --> 01:03:33.960
He's too busy peeing.

1434
01:03:34.380 --> 01:03:42.180
Speaking of outstanding feats of athleticism, back in the conference room, Dwight is beating Jim at ping pong.

1435
01:03:42.820 --> 01:03:46.740
Dwight looks like he's barely making any effort to beat Jim.

1436
01:03:46.900 --> 01:03:48.200
He's on his phone.

1437
01:03:48.200 --> 01:03:50.140
He can just hit the ball no matter what.

1438
01:03:50.320 --> 01:03:53.680
And Pam comes in at 16 minutes, 21 seconds.

1439
01:03:54.220 --> 01:03:55.820
Jenna, I'm going to show you your outfit.

1440
01:03:56.100 --> 01:03:57.460
I took a screen grab of it.

1441
01:03:57.600 --> 01:03:58.540
Oh, is it time?

1442
01:03:58.540 --> 01:04:03.360
It's time because this is the moment if you really want to see your outfit here.

1443
01:04:03.680 --> 01:04:04.900
What is this outfit?

1444
01:04:05.200 --> 01:04:07.040
What is your gripe about this outfit?

1445
01:04:07.220 --> 01:04:15.140
We have black tights, a long black skirt just below your knee, a blue buttoned up sweater.

1446
01:04:15.600 --> 01:04:21.820
I'm just saying, Pam went through this phase in the beginning of her courtship with Jim where she was zhuzhing herself up a little.

1447
01:04:22.020 --> 01:04:26.060
And I think this outfit says, I am now comfortable in my relationship.

1448
01:04:26.380 --> 01:04:27.700
Do with that what you will.

1449
01:04:27.700 --> 01:04:30.080
Well, I take it as an insult.

1450
01:04:30.960 --> 01:04:32.820
It seems phrased that way.

1451
01:04:34.560 --> 01:04:37.720
I think maybe Pam is just a little cold.

1452
01:04:37.960 --> 01:04:38.760
It's winter.

1453
01:04:39.120 --> 01:04:45.280
She has exchanged her pantyhose for tights, which is something we did for her in the winter months.

1454
01:04:45.280 --> 01:04:49.100
But I don't think she has gotten comfortable in any way.

1455
01:04:49.360 --> 01:04:53.660
She just had a day where maybe she didn't pair the right things together.

1456
01:04:53.660 --> 01:04:54.360
That is all.

1457
01:04:54.580 --> 01:04:55.760
I think you'll see.

1458
01:04:56.420 --> 01:05:00.860
In dinner party coming up, she's going to wear an adorable little cocktail dress.

1459
01:05:00.860 --> 01:05:01.640
So don't worry.

1460
01:05:02.120 --> 01:05:02.440
All right.

1461
01:05:02.560 --> 01:05:04.180
I will back off Pam's attire.

1462
01:05:04.380 --> 01:05:05.700
It did crack me up.

1463
01:05:05.980 --> 01:05:08.280
I have an observation about this scene.

1464
01:05:08.280 --> 01:05:11.240
Jim keeps saying spin serve.

1465
01:05:11.540 --> 01:05:12.560
Spin serve.

1466
01:05:12.980 --> 01:05:14.340
That is not a thing.

1467
01:05:14.720 --> 01:05:15.660
I looked it up.

1468
01:05:16.380 --> 01:05:19.760
Spin is not reserved for a serve.

1469
01:05:19.760 --> 01:05:26.920
And of the top five table tennis serves, only three of the five involve any spin.

1470
01:05:27.140 --> 01:05:28.420
And I'll tell you what they are.

1471
01:05:28.540 --> 01:05:31.700
Number one, the short side spin pendulum serve.

1472
01:05:32.140 --> 01:05:36.260
Number two, the short side spin reverse pendulum serve.

1473
01:05:36.260 --> 01:05:37.220
Mm-hmm.

1474
01:05:37.820 --> 01:05:41.360
Number three is the fast no spin cross serve.

1475
01:05:41.700 --> 01:05:45.860
Number four is the fast no spin down the line serve.

1476
01:05:46.500 --> 01:05:49.720
And number five is the long side spin serve.

1477
01:05:49.980 --> 01:05:53.460
So there's no such thing as just spin serve.

1478
01:05:53.720 --> 01:05:56.500
You have to be more specific, Jim.

1479
01:05:56.920 --> 01:05:58.440
Is it a short side spin?

1480
01:05:58.640 --> 01:05:59.460
Is it a pendulum?

1481
01:05:59.860 --> 01:06:01.520
Is it a long side spin?

1482
01:06:02.060 --> 01:06:06.760
And by the way, just because you're putting spin on it doesn't mean you're mastering the game

1483
01:06:06.760 --> 01:06:10.160
because there's a couple of great serves that involve no spin at all.

1484
01:06:10.380 --> 01:06:15.780
I don't know why I felt the need to put Jim in his place about this spin serve,

1485
01:06:15.920 --> 01:06:18.960
but he was just saying it with such authority.

1486
01:06:20.140 --> 01:06:21.680
And it's not a thing, Jim.

1487
01:06:21.800 --> 01:06:22.260
It's not a thing.

1488
01:06:22.920 --> 01:06:26.400
Well, don't tell my husband that because he thinks the spin serve is where it's at.

1489
01:06:26.660 --> 01:06:28.860
He's going to spin it on over.

1490
01:06:29.960 --> 01:06:33.720
Listen, it's just that there needs to be more specificity.

1491
01:06:33.960 --> 01:06:38.580
Well, I'm going to tell him that next time he's playing while I'm trying to eat at the ping-pong table.

1492
01:06:38.740 --> 01:06:39.240
Please do.

1493
01:06:39.320 --> 01:06:40.580
You put him in his place.

1494
01:06:40.860 --> 01:06:42.640
Josh, that's not the long spin.

1495
01:06:43.320 --> 01:06:44.140
Side spin.

1496
01:06:44.540 --> 01:06:45.340
Short spin.

1497
01:06:45.520 --> 01:06:46.520
That's a short spin.

1498
01:06:46.860 --> 01:06:47.660
There you go.

1499
01:06:48.160 --> 01:06:49.620
Well, Jim says he's ready.

1500
01:06:50.000 --> 01:06:51.580
I'm going to go play Daryl.

1501
01:06:51.700 --> 01:06:53.620
Pam's so excited they go down to the warehouse.

1502
01:06:53.620 --> 01:07:00.460
And right away, it's not going Jim's way, and Kelly starts doing all of her smack talk, trash talk.

1503
01:07:00.640 --> 01:07:01.720
She's making up a song.

1504
01:07:01.800 --> 01:07:04.220
Hey, hey, you, you, I don't like your boyfriend.

1505
01:07:04.560 --> 01:07:05.760
She's doing a whole thing.

1506
01:07:06.320 --> 01:07:07.140
Pam has had it.

1507
01:07:07.460 --> 01:07:07.780
Yeah.

1508
01:07:08.120 --> 01:07:12.300
She's like, you and me, right now, we're going to play.

1509
01:07:12.840 --> 01:07:14.280
And they are both horrible.

1510
01:07:14.800 --> 01:07:16.080
We had a fan question.

1511
01:07:23.520 --> 01:07:25.080
Let me say this.

1512
01:07:25.420 --> 01:07:29.720
Mindy and I were not great at ping-pong, but we weren't that bad at ping-pong.

1513
01:07:29.820 --> 01:07:33.340
So we were pretending to be that bad.

1514
01:07:33.440 --> 01:07:37.800
And the point was that we would never get past a certain number of volleys.

1515
01:07:37.940 --> 01:07:39.620
Are they called volleys in ping-pong?

1516
01:07:39.960 --> 01:07:41.140
You should know.

1517
01:07:42.040 --> 01:07:42.980
Ho, ho.

1518
01:07:43.240 --> 01:07:45.460
I didn't get that far in my research.

1519
01:07:47.220 --> 01:07:53.200
And also, I know that John was actually good at ping-pong, and he had to pretend to be not as good as he was.

1520
01:07:53.600 --> 01:07:53.860
Yes.

1521
01:07:54.140 --> 01:08:00.900
And by the way, we played a lot of ping-pong while we were just waiting for various camera setups and things like that.

1522
01:08:01.040 --> 01:08:08.380
This was a really fun day of shooting, actually, because we had this toy, which then stayed in the warehouse and we would play.

1523
01:08:08.760 --> 01:08:14.320
Well, and there are a lot of deleted scenes of all the other office members playing ping-pong that didn't make it in.

1524
01:08:14.320 --> 01:08:18.700
And Phyllis and Stanley play at the makeshift ping-pong table up in the conference room.

1525
01:08:18.860 --> 01:08:20.200
Meredith has a turn.

1526
01:08:20.480 --> 01:08:22.880
I yell off camera, I'm next!

1527
01:08:22.880 --> 01:08:26.440
So it's like everyone is getting in there to play ping-pong.

1528
01:08:27.160 --> 01:08:29.800
Lady, the deposition is almost over.

1529
01:08:29.800 --> 01:08:41.900
And it's so sad, because they've read David Wallace's testimony about whether or not he was ever really going to hire Michael for this corporate position.

1530
01:08:42.100 --> 01:08:50.460
